fix(smoke): the liveness guard called a training gate dead

Job 1677746 (oat-06, L40S sm_89). THE MODULE PASSED EVERY OPERATOR CHECK. Both
remaining failures were in the harness.

WHAT THE GPU CONFIRMED, and none of it was verifiable offline:

  fla honours activation=None       max|diff| vs 'silu' = 9.336e-01, not 0
  gated == plain at init, None      max|diff| = 0.000e+00
  gated == plain at init, silu      max|diff| = 0.000e+00
  conv path                         all 360 of 360 fused, both gated arms
  gate movement                     0.0212 and 0.0219 off neutral in 30 steps
  memory prediction vs measured     2.250 GiB vs 2.250 GiB

The first line is the one that could have collapsed the design: fla's kernel
matches its activation string exactly and ignores anything else without error,
so if it had ignored None too, kda-gated and kda-gated-silu would have been the
same model. It does not. The second and third close the gap where the arms'
bit-identical init was only ever checked on the reference path -- a path the
experiment never executes.

THE LIVENESS GUARD WAS WRONG, AND ITS OWN RUN PROVED IT. It failed
kda-gated-silu at a gradient of 9.477e-06 against a floor of 1.043e-05, on the
same run where that arm's gate MOVED 0.0219 off neutral -- more than the passing
arm's 0.0212. A guard that calls an arm dead while the run watches it train is a
defect in the guard, not conservatism.

The threshold was relative to another parameter's gradient. That is the wrong
denominator under an adaptive optimizer: AdamW normalizes each parameter by its
own second moment, so the step is lr*m/(sqrt(v)+eps) and a uniformly smaller
gradient takes the same step. A gradient's ratio to some other parameter's
gradient does not enter the update at all. Only two things make a parameter
untrainable: an exactly-zero gradient, or one small enough that eps dominates
sqrt(v). So the floor is now absolute, 10x AdamW's eps. reference_grad is still
taken and still used to fail closed, because a dead reference means the harness
is not measuring anything.

A test pins it: the same gate gradient must give the same verdict for references
spanning 1e-3 to 1e6. Under the old rule the 1e6 case fails.

Also: the gated-conv count keyed on 'kda-plain' exactly, so it failed
kda-plain-noact for being the control it is -- left over from the three-arm
version. And the memory prediction is now a GATE rather than a printed number,
since it matched to three decimal places; a prediction that drifts low is what
gets a paid run OOM-killed.

Measured cost of the depthwise gate at d=2048, T=4096, bf16, eager: peak memory
2.929 -> 5.179 GiB, a 1.768x increase for 49,152 parameters (0.0687%). The
parameter delta is not the cost, and now there is a measurement saying so.

66 CPU tests, 7 fla-gated. 19 mutations, 0 survived, 0 no-op.

